# Never commit real secrets or per-environment config into the toolkit.
# Templates live in examples/*.example (kept).
*-token
*.env
.env*
.kanban-*
# Python bytecode. Both routes that were minting it are closed: the bin/*.py entry points
# (card#6871 — bin/ is symlinked onto PATH entry by entry, so a __pycache__ there becomes a
# PATH entry), and CI's own syntax gate (card#7207 — it ran py_compile, which writes the
# bytecode it produces; it now uses compile(), which does not). A HAND-RUN of py_compile still
# mints one, and this keeps that out of the index.
# ⚠ An ignore is not an absence: an ignored directory in bin/ is invisible to `git status`
# while still being globbed by every consumer of bin/*. That is what card#6871 was.
__pycache__/
*.pyc
# .release-pr.json IS committed (non-secret project config: board id + public API
# base + artifact list) — same as agent-webhook-bridge and kanban-board. It drives
# bin/release-pr-body + bin/promote-released-cards and must be in-repo for the
# release tooling to run reproducibly (incl. CI).

# Editor, backup and packaging siblings of ANY file, secret-bearing ones included. A
# backup carries the same secret as the file it copies, and a per-file enumeration is
# permanently one editor behind: `.env` was ignored here while `.env~`, `auth.json~`
# and `storage/*.key~` were all stageable (roundtable #316, found live on a peer
# install as a 4,339-byte `.env~`). The rules above name WHICH files are secret;
# these cover HOW a tool spells a copy of one, so a new secret file added above
# inherits the coverage instead of needing its own siblings enumerated.
#
# ⚑ ONE BLOCK OVER EVERY FILE, NOT ONE BLOCK PER SECRET RULE — that is the whole point.
# Four rules above name secret-bearing files; giving each its own sibling list is four
# places to forget the fifth affix, which is exactly how the first pass of this fix
# shipped without `*.swp` and left `auth.json.swp` stageable.
#
# ⛔ THIS LIST IS A LOWER BOUND AND CANNOT BE COMPLETED. nano writes `.save.1`, `.save.2`
# … without limit and `cp .env .env.for-alice` invents a suffix on the spot; gitignore has
# no "any backup of this" concept to express that. A rule spelled with a trailing `*`
# (`.env*`, `.kanban-*` above) is the only construction that covers its whole suffix family
# unconditionally — prefer it wherever it does not over-widen. `bin/gitignore-secret-family-check`
# holds this block level with the affix table it derives; every entry below was taken from
# its producer's own documentation or measured on a real box, never recalled.
*~
.#*
# the next line is emacs's auto-save spelling `#<name>#`; the leading `#` MUST be
# backslash-escaped or gitignore reads the whole line as a comment and the rule
# silently does nothing (measured — it is why this needed a second pass).
\#*#
*.bak
*.bak-*
*.bak.*
*.orig
*.rej
*.save
*.save.[0-9]*
*.sw[a-p]
*.old
*.tmp
*.copy
*.dpkg-old
*.dpkg-dist
*.dpkg-new
*.rpmsave
*.rpmnew
*.ucf-old
*.ucf-dist
*.ucf-new
*.BACKUP.[0-9]*
*.LOCAL.[0-9]*
*.REMOTE.[0-9]*
*.BASE.[0-9]*
